+This setting controls browser-initiated logout, independently of back-channel
+logout below.
+
+### Provider-initiated back-channel logout
+
+Set `backchannelLogout: true` on a provider and register this URL at the IdP:
+
+```
+https://requests.example.com/api/v1/auth/oidc/backchannel/authentik
+```
+
+Replace the hostname and provider slug for your installation. In Authentik,
+select **Back-channel** as the provider's logout method and set its logout URI
+to that URL. The endpoint accepts form-encoded `logout_token` POSTs without
+browser cookies or CSRF tokens; the signed JWT authenticates the request.
+
+Tokens are verified against the configured issuer's discovered signing keys,
+audience, signature algorithm, expiration, issuance time (at most five minutes
+old, with five seconds of clock tolerance), logout event, and required claims.
+A nonce is forbidden. Encrypted logout tokens are not supported. Discovery and
+JWKS retrieval each have a three-second timeout. No raw tokens are logged.
+
+New OIDC sessions remember the verified ID token's issuer, client ID, subject,
+issuance time, and optional `sid`. Persistent database revocation markers are
+checked before authenticating every session-backed API request, and before
+completing an OIDC callback. A `sid` logout targets that IdP session; when both
+`sid` and `sub` are supplied, both must match. A subject-only logout revokes all
+of that subject's sessions whose ID tokens were issued at or before the event.
+Freshly issued tokens in the same second are conservatively rejected too.
+Markers survive process restarts and session-save races. Repeated events are
+idempotent, and older events cannot move a revocation timestamp backwards.
+
+Old sessions without this metadata cannot be correlated safely: users must sign
+in again, or operators must explicitly clear existing Seerr sessions at rollout.
+Local sessions and API-key authentication are not revoked by OIDC logout.
+The SQLite and PostgreSQL migrations add the revocation table; do not remove
+its rows while corresponding sessions or in-flight login tokens could exist.
+
+This still depends on the IdP delivering an event. In particular, Authentik may
+only dispatch logout against unexpired provider tokens: test fresh sessions,
+sessions older than the access-token lifetime, and restart behavior in your
+actual deployment. Account disabling and group changes are not continuously
+polled, and browser UI updates wait until its next API request.
 
